    {
      "path": "lib/ui/features/settings/views/model_settings_view.dart",
      "content": "Each `_SettingsSection` frames its content with both a top and a bottom rule (`Border.symmetric(horizontal: ...)`), while `_SettingsLedger` still separates adjacent sections with a `spaceLg` gap. Between two neighboring sections this renders two parallel horizontal lines (previous section's bottom border, blank gap + group title, next section's top border), e.g. between \"外观与语言\" and \"新会议默认\", and between every pair in the right-hand ledger. The requirement calls for shared edges/dividers (共享边缘和分隔线), and the old code drew exactly one rule per section boundary via `topRule`. Please confirm the double rule is intentional; if not, consider drawing only a top border per section (or collapsing the ledger spacing so borders coincide) so adjacent sections share a single edge.",
      "existing_code": "        DecoratedBox(\n          decoration: BoxDecoration(\n            border: Border.symmetric(\n              horizontal: BorderSide(\n                color: theme.colors.border,\n                width: appStyle.dividerWidth,\n              ),\n            ),\n          ),\n          child: child,\n        ),",
      "start_line": 1048,
      "end_line": 1058,
      "thinking": "Good — a 2.0x scaling test exists and covers that case. Test coverage seems to be sufficiently in place.\n\nSo now, moving on to finalize. Beyond the two confirmed findings, the only substantive concern I have is the double horizontal rule between adjacent sections. Let me take one more very careful look at `_SettingsSection` and `_SettingsLedger` to see whether that's really the case, and also consider the border of the DecoratedBox for the appearance/language merge.\n\n`_SettingsSection.build`:\n```dart\nColumn(\n  crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.stretch,\n  children: [\n    Text(title, ...),\n    SizedBox(height: appStyle.spaceXs),\n    DecoratedBox(\n      decoration: BoxDecoration(\n        border: Border.symmetric(horizontal: BorderSide(...)),\n      ),\n      child: child,\n    ),\n  ],\n)\n```\n\nEach section's content block is framed by a top border and a bottom border. `_SettingsLedger` inserts `spaceLg` between sections. So between section A and section B, you get: A's bottom border, spaceLg, B's title, spaceXs, B's top border. Two horizontal lines sandwiching the group title. Compared to the old design, which had a single `topRule` per section boundary, this is a substantive visual change.\n\nIs this wrong? The requirements say: \"mobile-end compact native settings list, small group titles, shared edges and divider lines\" (移动端紧凑原生设置列表、小分组标题、共享边缘和分隔线). \"共享边缘\" — shared edges — suggests that adjacent sections should share a single divider line. With this implementation, each section has its own top and bottom edge, and there's spacing between sections. Hmm, but it could also be said to be an acceptable interpretation of \"group boxes.\"\n\nHmm, one thing to note: in the old diff's thesis comment (which was removed), it said \"连续分区、细规则线\" (continuous partitions, thin rule lines). The deleted design intent said \"continuous partitions\" — with continuous partitions, adjacent sections share an edge. If the new code is separating sections with a spaceLg gap and double borders, that's no longer \"continuous.\" That said, the thesis comment was deleted, so the author may have intentionally shifted the design toward something closer to a native settings list where each group is independent.\n\nHonestly, without seeing the screenshot I can't definitively say this is a bug. But this is the only structural concern I can identify, and it's worth asking about. I'll raise it as a low-severity \"other\" comment.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider whether it's really double lines. In `_SettingsLedger` the sections are separated by `spaceLg` spacing. The bottom border of section A is drawn at the bottom of A's DecoratedBox, and the top border of section B is drawn above B's content. Between them: spaceLg + B's title + spaceXs. So there are 2 lines with the group title sandwiched between the previous line and the next one. Visually: line, blank, title, line, content. Hmm, this is a common pattern in \"ledger\" designs (like a bank statement, which matches \"账本\"): each entry is framed by rules. It may well be intentional. Even so, I'll phrase it softly as a question.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time whether there's any other concrete issue…\n\nLet me look at `_body` again:\n\n```dart\nfinal localSections = \u003cWidget\u003e[\n  _OfflineResourcesSection(...),\n  if (widget.dataControls case final dataControls?)\n    ListenableBuilder(... _StoragePrivacySection ...),\n  if (widget.remoteDiagnostics case final remoteDiagnostics?)\n    ListenableBuilder(... _RemoteDiagnosticsSection ...),\n  if (widget.dataControls case final dataControls?)\n    ListenableBuilder(... _DiagnosticsSection ...),\n];\nfinal dataControls = widget.dataControls;\n```\n\nThe local variable `dataControls` is declared after `localSections`. Not a problem but a slightly odd ordering; `localSections` uses `widget.dataControls` directly. Cosmetic issue.\n\nNow, the `hasDataSections` variable inside the builder uses `dataControls != null || widget.remoteDiagnostics != null` — matches the old condition. Good.\n\nOK, next let me also double-check that the test file's changes aren't missing `find.text('浅色')`/`'深色'` expectations that could now falsely pass because the menu is closed. In the theme test: initially 浅色/深色 are findsNothing (because the menu is closed), then open the menu and tap dark, and after saving expect `find.text('深色')` findsOneWidget — that's the details text displaying the current mode label. Good.\n\nOne more potential problem scenario with `managedRadio` + key trick: after a successful save, `selectedMode` = new mode, key = `appearance-setting-row-dark`. If the user later changes the theme from somewhere else back to system (via the app's themeMode notifier), the ListenableBuilder rebuilds, key = `...-system`, new control. Good.\n\nOK — I'll also re-verify that in the test's language rollback test, `languageMode.value` remains `AppLanguageMode.english` after the failed save. Yes, the view model rolls back. Good.\n\nFinal decision: there is one comment worth raising (the double-rule border between sections). I should also double-check whether the merged appearance-language `_SettingsSection` + nested `DecoratedBox` bottom border produces a double bottom line when `languageSettings == null` — no, in that case the border is null.\n\nAlso, when both are present, the bottom border of the appearance DecoratedBox sits between appearance and language within the framed block — a single shared line. Consistent.\n\nLet me also reconsider `_ModelMaintenanceMenu` — a DecoratedBox with a `Border(top:)` without dividerWidth — pre-existing (unchanged in the diff? let me check). Looking at the diff, `_ModelMaintenanceMenu` was not modified except that `_DataLedger` was removed. The top border of `_ModelMaintenanceMenu` uses default width 1 rather than `appStyle.dividerWidth` — pre-existing, unchanged code. Skip.\n\nAlright, I'll submit the one comment regarding the double-border/spacing concern, and note it as low severity since it may be intentional. Actually, hmm, let me weigh this: the instructions say to avoid commenting on subjective design matters… but the requirement background explicitly cites \"共享边缘和分隔线\" (shared edges and divider lines) as a goal, and this structural choice directly contradicts it by producing paired rules. This is a substantive review point tied to the stated requirement, not mere aesthetic nitpicking. I'll include it as low-to-medium severity. I'll go with \"medium\" — actually no, since it may be intentional, \"low\" feels more appropriate. I'll settle on low severity, category \"maintainability\" or \"other\". I'll use \"other\".\n\nI'll write the comment targeting the `_SettingsLedger` code (separator) and the `_SettingsSection` border. The most relevant new code is probably inside `_SettingsSection`. I'll attach it to the `Border.symmetric` block.",
      "category": "other",
      "severity": "low"
    }
